      "writtenOn": "2026-07-27T07:58:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Follow-up: IB PortCounters defines port_rcv_data/port_xmit_data\nas data octets divided by 4, so could you check whether the device already normalizes them?",
      "parentUuid": "3209d65e_6ce5fe0d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 162,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 163,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "64c9ebde522ba44fd19d17ed7e7ccfcd72cf74bf",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4a2c9922_c02f5ea8",
        "filename": "src/nvidia-gpu/NvidiaIbPortMetrics.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002810
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-26T07:57:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. Done — rx_bytes/tx_bytes now use Unit.Bytes \n2. Our platform cannot produce traffic on an InfiniBand port, so every\n   0x01 counter reads 0 and the device behaviour is not observable\n   here. NSM Type 1 section 2.3.7 defines these counters by reference\n   to the InfiniBand specification section 16.1.3.5 PortCounters, where\n   Table 191 defines PortRcvData / PortXmitData as the total number of\n   data octets divided by four:\n\n   https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-ipoib-perf-mgmt-agent-mib-03\n   (quotes IBA Vol.1 section 16.1.3.5, Table 191 verbatim)\n\n   So PS8 multiplies counters 1 and 9 by four before publishing them as\n   Unit.Bytes.",
      "parentUuid": "80152e43_ee4b83e3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 162,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 163,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "64c9ebde522ba44fd19d17ed7e7ccfcd72cf74bf",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
